The quadratic formula can be used to find the solutions to the quadratic equation:

\begin{equation*}
ax^2+bx+c=0
\end{equation*}

The formula is:

\begin{equation*}
x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}
\end{equation*}

where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are coefficients of the quadratic equation.

Therefore, the solutions to the quadratic equation are:

\begin{equation*}
x_1 = \frac{-b + \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
x_2 = \frac{-b - \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}
\end{equation*}

Note that the expression inside the square root, $b^2 - 4ac$, is called the discriminant. If the discriminant is negative, the quadratic equation has no real solutions, but it may have complex solutions. If the discriminant is zero, the quadratic equation has one real solution. If the discriminant is positive, the quadratic equation has two real solutions.

```r
find_intersection_point <- function(vector1, vector2) {
  # Extract coordinates from vectors
  x1 <- vector1[1]
  y1 <- vector1[2]
  x2 <- vector2[1]
  y2 <- vector2[2]

  # Calculate determinant of the matrix
  det <- x1 * y2 - x2 * y1

  # Check if determinant is zero, which means the vectors are parallel
  if (det == 0) {
    return(NULL)
  }

  # Calculate the intersection point
  x <- (x2 * y1 - x1 * y2) / det
  y <- (-y2 * x1 + x2 * y1) / det

  # Return intersection point as a vector
  return(c(x, y))
}
```

Using an open source C++ library for converting Markdown to HTML would be a more robust solution.  An example of a C++ library that could be used for this is maddy. maddy is an MIT licensed header-only library that only depends on the C++ 14 standard library.

The `markdownToHtml` function could be reimplemented using the maddy library as follows:

```
#include <memory>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

#include "maddy/parser.h"

std::string markdownToHtml(std::string& markdown) {
    std::stringstream markdownStream(markdown);
    auto parser = std::make_unique<maddy::Parser>();
    return parser->Parse(markdownStream);
}
```

To compile this code you will also need to add the headers for the "maddy" library to the include path. Depending on the compiler, build system or IDE being used, the process to change the include path may vary.

As an alternative you could also a C library such as MD4C. MD4C is an MIT licensed Markdown parser written in C. It is compliant to the CommonMark specification.
